Run the Extension Host as a worker_thread instead of a forked process.

Completes the pair started with the Pty Host. The Extension Host is the
longest-lived fork the server makes, and on Android 12+ every fork spends one of
32 system-wide background process slots.

It needs more than the Pty Host did, because it hands the client's socket to the
child over IPC and a worker_thread has no channel that can carry a handle.
_canSendSocket is therefore forced off, which selects the named-pipe path VS Code
already uses on Windows: the host connects back over a pipe and the server
bridges the two sockets itself, with no handle ever crossing the IPC boundary.

That is not quite sufficient, and the gap is not in the plan this follows.
_sendSocketToExtensionHost is unreachable on the initial connection once the flag
is off, but acceptReconnection() calls it without checking the flag at all. Left
alone, send(msg, socket) would report success while dropping the socket, and the
host would wait for a connection that never arrives with nothing logged. It now
refuses and says so, naming the process in the message -- which is also what
keeps that parameter referenced once the body that used it is gone. Reconnecting
after a WebView recreation consequently needs a fresh connection rather than a
resumed one; that is a real limitation, recorded here rather than discovered
later.

